Three Weaknesses: 
1. Requirements aren't writeen down - They're passed verbally and developers sometimes have to guess.
2. There is no code review - Developers push code straight to the main brand.
3. There isn't enough testing - The developer who made the feature only tests it themselves once.



Task 3:

Weakness 1: Requirements aren't written down.
No process at all: The team has no shared written requirements and relies on the team lead remembering what the client said. Even though lightweight processes use less documentation than heavyweight processes, this doesn't mean having no documentation at all. This can easily lead to developers misunderstanding what the client actually wants.

Weakness 2: There is no code review.
No process at all: Code is pushed straight to the main branch without another developer reviewing it first. THis means mistakes could easily be missed and end up in the finished system. Even a lightweight process should still have some checks in place to make sure the code works properly.

Weakness 3: There isn't enough testing.
Agile/Lightweight process would catch this: The developer only tests their own feature once before moving on. Agile still focuses on testing and delivering working software, so testing should be done properly and regularly instead of just checking the feature once.



Task 4:

1. Requirements aren't written down - MUST
The team should use a simple shared document to write down the client's requirements so everyone knows exactly what needs to be done. I would make this a MUST because misunderstandings in requirements have already caused features to be delivered late.

2. No code reviews - SHOULD
Before code is added to the main branch, another developer should briefly review it. I would make this a SHOULD because it could catch mistakes before they make it into the finished system.

3. Not enough testing - COULD
The team could introduce a simple testing checklist so that each feature is properly tested before moving on. I would make this a could because it would improve the quality of the software without costing much to introduce.


